"""Story-rhyme cards — the shared cultural reference repertoire.

These are the seven story cards of the Fellowship's shared
reference repertoire, carried into ordinary Talk for the eligible
seats when the reference carriage is on (see
``_SHARED_REFERENCE_GUIDANCE``, ``_reference_carriage_lines`` and
``fox_rhyme_offer_enabled`` in ``local_ai/conversation/reply.py``,
which gate the assembly). This module is inert data plus one
formatter: all gating lives at the reply.py assembly site, so with
the flag off the cards never reach a prompt, and the meeting path
never sees them.

Source: ``~/workspace/goals/seven-seat-worlds/files/zenaf-stock-root-repertoire-draft-2026-11-20.md``
— the card texts below (attribution line plus the four labeled
parts: What happens / What shape it makes / What might fit / Where
it breaks) are extracted verbatim from that document. They are the
2026-11-20 corrected cards: fidelity-audited, with
founder-directed primary-source corrections applied (Grimm 1812
kept distinct from 1857, Ovid distinct from Apollodorus, Homer
distinct from the later mythographers). By founder verdict
(2026-11-20) these seven cards are ADMITTED TO CANON as the
founding sample of the shared cultural repertoire — one
founder-governed archive that all Fellowship seats may draw from,
interpreted through each seat's own perspective. Admission
establishes reference fidelity only: a card is shared reference
material, never evidence about a user and never an accepted
interpretation of anyone's life. If the source document's cards
are corrected again, re-extract; do not edit the texts here
independently of the source.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

# Framing for the block as it rides in the prompt, immediately after
# the shared reference guidance. The cards speak for themselves; the
# framing only fixes their standing: curated references, held in
# pencil, offered under the discipline, breaks included.
_FRAMING = (
    "Story cards you carry — curated references, in pencil. Offer "
    "from them only as the discipline above allows, and let each "
    "card's \"Where it breaks\" travel with the offer."
)

RHYME_CARDS: tuple[tuple[str, str], ...] = (
    ('Sisyphus',
     """**Sisyphus** — Greek myth. Homer, *Odyssey* 11, for the punishment; the death-cheating belongs to later mythographic accounts (Apollodorus, *Library* 1.9.3; Hyginus, *Fabulae* 60; the binding of Death is traced to Pherecydes). Albert Camus, *The Myth of Sisyphus* (1942) for the modern reading.
**What happens (Homer, *Odyssey* 11):** Odysseus, visiting the underworld, sees Sisyphus straining to push a monstrous boulder up a hill with both hands; each time it nears the top, its weight turns it and it rolls back down to the plain, and he braces himself and begins again. Homer gives no crime, no backstory, no explanation — only the labor, endless.
**What happens (the wider myth, later accounts):** In the tradition the mythographers record, Sisyphus is the man who cheated death twice. In Apollodorus's account he betrays Zeus's secret to the river-god Asopus, and when Death is sent for him he binds Death fast, so that no one can die until Death is freed and claims him; but before dying he has ordered his wife to leave his corpse unburied, and in the underworld he persuades Persephone to let him return to the living to set the burial right — then refuses to go back, lives out his days, and is finally dragged below by force. It is this later tradition, not Homer, that makes the boulder a punishment for the trickery.
**What shape it makes:** (Interpretation) Effort that resets; labor whose product is the labor itself. Camus's reading: the walk back down, in full consciousness of the futility, is the hour in which Sisyphus is stronger than his rock — "one must imagine Sisyphus happy." That is Camus's philosophical stance (scorn, lucidity), contested as philosophy, not a settled meaning of the myth.
**What might fit:** A person describes work that undoes itself; the same task returning unchanged, over and over; effort they say never accumulates into anything kept.
**Where it breaks:** In the wider myth the sentence was earned by trickery — Sisyphus is punished for cheating death, not an innocent labourer (Homer himself assigns no blame at all). An offer may compare the labor aspect only; it must not imply the visitor is being punished, or deserves to be. It also fits badly where effort is in fact accumulating invisibly, and Camus's happiness is a stance, not a promise. Never offer it as a verdict that the work is pointless."""),
    ("The miller's daughter (Rumpelstiltskin)",
     """**The miller's daughter (Rumpelstiltskin)** — Brothers Grimm, "Rumpelstilzchen," *Children's and Household Tales*. This card follows the **1812** edition; the famous ending in which the imp tears himself in two is from the **1857** edition and is flagged in "Where it breaks."
**What happens:** A miller boasts to the king that his daughter can spin straw into gold. The king locks her in a room full of straw and orders her to spin it by morning or die; each night the room and the straw are larger. A little man appears and does the spinning — the first night for her necklace, the second for the ring from her finger, the third for her promise of her first child if she becomes queen. She does become queen; the king marries her for the gold. When the little man comes for the child, he refuses her offer of all the kingdom's riches — and himself offers a reprieve: if she can guess his name within three days, she keeps the child. On the third day a messenger she sent out overhears him singing his name beside a fire; she names him, and in 1812 he runs away angrily and never comes back.
**What shape it makes:** (Interpretation) An impossible demand met through a series of priced bargains, each costing more of what she has; a release that comes half from the helper's own offered terms and half from learning the hidden name.
**What might fit:** A person says they are ordered to produce something valuable out of nothing, on pain of losing everything; that they got through an impossible demand only because someone or something else did the crucial part for them; that the help was agreed under pressure, at a price the helper set; that a bargain they made to survive one crisis has left a cost that is now coming due.
**Where it breaks:** The daughter never spins, in any version, and she has no spinning gift to be credited with — the work is entirely the little man's, obtained by bargain. This card must never be used to suggest that the visitor's own gift secretly did the work, nor that the visitor's gift is not theirs: the rhyme lives in the impossible demand, the borrowed hands, the coercive bargain, and the deferred price — nowhere else. The king is no neutral demander: he threatens her with death three times and marries her for the gold; mapping an employer or patron onto him imports an extortionist. The firstborn promise is extracted under duress, agreed to as a contingency she thinks will never arise — the tale can unfairly imply the visitor carelessly promised something precious away. Endings differ by edition: 1812, he runs away and never returns; 1857, he tears himself in two; the oral version the Grimms collected has him fly out of the window on a cooking ladle. Never narrate one edition's ending as another's."""),
    ('Daedalus',
     """**Daedalus** — Greek myth. The two principal sources disagree about the detention; they are given separately, not blended.
**What happens (Ovid, *Metamorphoses* Book 8):** Daedalus, an exile on Crete, is detained by King Minos, who blocks his escape by land and sea. He makes wings of feathers fastened with thread and wax and escapes by air; on the way his son falls, and Daedalus buries him on the island that takes the boy's name, then reaches Sicily, where King Cocalus takes up arms in his defence.
**What happens (Apollodorus, *Epitome*):** Minos, enraged that Daedalus gave Ariadne the thread that defeated the Labyrinth Daedalus himself built, shuts Daedalus and Icarus inside the Labyrinth; they escape on wings, and Daedalus reaches Sicily, sheltered by King Cocalus.
**What shape it makes:** (Interpretation) The maker escapes a trap his own work made, or made possible, by his craft — and the escape itself carries the cost.
**What might fit:** A person says their skill is the only way out of a situation they are in; that they are stuck inside a system they helped build; that they have a plan that is a genuine escape and that they know will cost something real.
**Where it breaks:** The cost of Daedalus's escape falls on someone else — his son, whose risk he knew in advance; in Ovid he fits the wings with tears and trembling hands. His complicity is also darker than "he built the prison": he armed his own Labyrinth's defeat with Ariadne's thread, and in Ovid's immediately following episode he had earlier murdered his nephew and apprentice Perdix out of envy of the boy's inventiveness. The story's center is grief and survival, not triumph; it fits a maker with clean hands badly."""),
    ('Icarus',
     """**Icarus** — Greek myth (Ovid, *Metamorphoses* Book 8; Apollodorus agrees on the events narrated here).
**What happens:** Daedalus, his father, fits him with wings and warns him to fly a middle course: too low, and the sea's moisture will weigh the feathers down; too high, and the sun will melt the wax. Flying beside his father on their escape from Crete, Icarus delights in the flight, climbs, the wax melts, and he falls into the sea.
**What shape it makes:** (Interpretation) A gift with a limit built into its own material; a fall that comes from the height the gift made possible, not from any failure of effort.
**What might fit:** A person describes a strength they know has a ceiling written into it; exhilaration or momentum carrying them past a limit they had been warned about; a setback that came from reaching, not from slacking.
**Where it breaks:** The warning was a band, not a ban — the low road was warned against too — but the sources show only the high road killing him; do not state as source fact that flying low was equally fatal. Icarus is also a child disobeying his father, on an escape flight rather than a venture freely chosen for gain: offered carelessly, the rhyme casts the visitor as the disobedient child and some authority as the wise father, and imports a hubris moral the escape context does not support. Never offer it as a warning against ambition."""),
    ('Bricks without straw',
     """**Bricks without straw** — Exodus 5 (Hebrew Bible).
**What happens:** Moses has just asked Pharaoh to let the Israelites go and hold a festival in the wilderness. Pharaoh answers by ordering that the enslaved Israelites no longer be given straw, while their daily brick quota stays exactly the same — "the number of bricks that they made in the past you shall impose on them, you shall by no means reduce it, for they are idle." Ordered to gather straw for themselves, the scattered people in fact gather stubble. When the quota fails, the Israelite foremen are beaten; protesting to Pharaoh, they are told to their faces: "You are idle, you are idle."
**What shape it makes:** (Interpretation) The demand stays fixed while the means are withdrawn — and degraded: the same count, from worse material, under an accusation of laziness.
**What might fit:** A person says they are required to keep producing the same output after the resources for it were withdrawn; that their tools or support got worse while the target did not move; that a shortfall is being attributed to their laziness or attitude.
**Where it breaks:** This is the enslavement of a people inside a liberation narrative, not an individual's workload: the rhyme is in the quota and the accusation, not a prediction of deliverance, and mapping it onto one person's job casts an employer as Pharaoh — a proportionality risk to name, not hide. It is also a text many visitors hold sacred; offer it only where the fit is in the conditions the visitor states, not the theology. ("Impoverished regardless of effort" is a condition a modern visitor may state; the chapter itself is about unpaid slaves — never present wages as a feature of the source.)"""),
    ("Psyche's tasks",
     """**Psyche's tasks** — Apuleius, *Metamorphoses* Books 4–6 (2nd century CE), the tale of Cupid and Psyche.
**What happens:** Venus, to destroy Psyche and keep her from Cupid, has her beaten and sets her four tasks. (1) Sort a heap of mixed grains before evening — a swarm of ants does it for her. (2) Bring wool from the golden-fleeced, violent rams by the river — a reed on the riverbank advises her to wait until the rams rest in the afternoon heat, then gather the wool caught on the thorns and briars; Psyche gathers it herself. (3) Fill a crystal vessel with water from the spring that feeds the Styx, high on a cliff and guarded by dragons — Jupiter's eagle takes the vessel and fills it. (4) Descend to the underworld and bring back a box of Proserpina's beauty — a speaking tower gives her the instructions; she completes the descent, but on the way back opens the box, finds not beauty but infernal sleep, and falls deathlike until Cupid revives her.
**What shape it makes:** (Interpretation) An impossible workload carried by a mixed pattern of help: some of it done for her (ants, eagle), some of it done by her on received counsel (reed, tower) — help received rather than seized. "Psyche" is Greek for *soul*, and the tale carries an ancient allegorical reading as the soul's trials and union with love.
**What might fit:** A person describes a workload no single pair of hands could finish; help arriving from quarters they had not considered; being handed task after task by someone who seems to want them to fail.
**Where it breaks:** The tasks are punitive servitude — Venus sets them to destroy Psyche; offered for an ordinary overload situation, the rhyme imports a persecutor. Psyche also fails a trust twice (the lamp, the box), and the story's resolution is rescue and divinisation, not the workload becoming manageable: the condition of being tasked ends by escape, not by sustainable completion. "Worth proven by endurance" is an interpretation sometimes laid on this tale, not a fact of it and not a fit signal — her worth is recognised through the completed tasks and Cupid's love."""),
    ('Cinderella',
     """**Cinderella** — two distinct versions, given separately; never blend them. Perrault, "Cendrillon," *Histoires ou contes du temps passé* (1697); Brothers Grimm, "Aschenputtel," *Children's and Household Tales*, **1812 first edition** (later editions change the tale materially — see "Where it breaks").
**What happens (Perrault, 1697):** A girl kept in menial labor by her stepmother and stepsisters is dressed for the prince's ball by her fairy godmother, on condition she return by midnight. She attends two balls; fleeing the second at midnight, she leaves behind a glass slipper. The slipper is tried on the kingdom's maidens; at her own home it fits her, and she produces its match. She forgives the stepsisters and marries them to two lords of the court.
**What happens (Grimm, 1812):** On her deathbed her mother gives her the means herself: "Plant a little tree on my grave, and when you wish for something, shake it, and you shall have it — and if you are in need, I will send you help; only stay pious and good." She plants the tree — the 1812 text names no kind of tree, and there is no twig brought home by her father — and her tears water it. The king gives a ball to last three days, at which his son is to choose a bride. Each evening the stepsisters set her a sorting task — a bowl of lentils, then a sack of vetches, then a bowl of peas — and each time two white doves fly in, sort the good from the bad in minutes ("the bad ones into our little crop, the good ones into the pot"), and tell her what to do next. On the **first** night she does not attend at all: the doves send her up onto the dovecote to watch her sisters dance with the prince. On the second and third nights the doves send her to the tree; she shakes it — "Little tree, rattle yourself and shake yourself, throw beautiful clothes down for me!" — and the clothes fall from the tree (a silver dress with silver slippers the first night; a dress all of gold and gems with golden slippers the second), a carriage with six horses waits at the door, and when she comes home the tree takes the clothes back and she dirties her face and sleeps in the ashes. Both nights she must be home before midnight. On the third night the prince, determined to learn who the unknown "princess" is, posts watchmen on the street and has the staircase smeared with pitch; dancing, she forgets the hour, hears the clock strike, and flees, leaving a golden slipper stuck fast in the pitch — and at the twelfth stroke the carriage vanishes and she stands in her ash-clothes on the dark street. The prince proclaims that whoever the golden slipper fits shall be his wife. It fits no one. Before either stepsister tries it, their mother secretly hands both of them a knife: "If the slipper is still too tight for you, cut a piece off your foot — it hurts a little, but what does it matter, it soon passes, and one of you will be queen." The **eldest** tries first: her toes go in but her heel is too big, so she cuts a piece off her **heel** and forces the foot in; the prince takes her for his bride, but at the gate the doves sing "Blood is in the shoe — the shoe is too small — the right bride is still at home," and he sees the blood welling out and brings her back. The mother then tells the **second** sister, if it is too short, to cut at the front, at the **toes**; she bites her teeth together, cuts a large piece off her toes, and is exposed the same way, the doves singing and her white stockings dyed red. Only then is Aschenputtel called, at the prince's insistence. She first washes her face and hands fresh and clean. The golden slipper fits her foot as if cast for it — and as she straightens up, the prince looks into her face and **recognizes the beautiful princess he had danced with**: "That is the right bride." The doves' final song confirms her — "No blood in the shoe; the right bride he is taking home" — as he drives her away. The tale ends there: in 1812 the stepsisters receive no further punishment.
**What shape it makes:** (Interpretation) Worth hidden under menial labor, identified in the end by the thing that fits only her — and then, in Grimm, known by her face. The versions rhyme differently: Perrault's splendour is borrowed and expires at midnight; Grimm's finery comes from the tree she planted on her mother's grave at her dying mother's own instruction.
**What might fit:** A person says their real capability is buried under servitude-grade work; that the household or workplace they serve does not see them; that they have a gift or craft they could be known by, if it were ever seen.
**Where it breaks:** In neither version is she recognised at the festival — the slipper trial is a separate, later procedure, held at her home. Keep identification and recognition distinct: in Grimm (1812) the slipper *identifies* her — it fits her alone, after two false fits are exposed by blood — and only then does the prince, looking into her washed face, *recognize* the princess he danced with. Do not retell it as recognition by the slipper alone; but do not overcorrect either — without the slipper he could not have found her: at the ball he never learns her name or her house, and at home her face is deliberately dirtied with ash. Grimm's festival also runs three nights, not one ball, and she attends only the last two, watching the first from the dovecote. Later editions are different tales in these very details: the hazel tree and the twig her father breaks off for her, the bird in the tree throwing down wishes, and the doves pecking out the stepsisters' eyes at the wedding are all additions from 1819 onward — never narrate them as 1812. Grimm's source is harsher than the greeting-card version (the cut feet are already in 1812), Perrault's gentler; name which version is meant, or don't offer it. The Perrault godmother-and-marriage version will be heard whether offered or not — the most culturally flattened story in this set."""),
)


def rhyme_cards_block() -> str:
    """The full cards block for the prompt: framing line, then each
    card's verbatim text, separated by blank lines."""
    parts = [_FRAMING]
    parts.extend(text for _name, text in RHYME_CARDS)
    return "\n\n".join(parts)
